---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { EDGES, CITIES, FACULTY, HAS_DATES, HAS_REGISTRATION } from '../data/program';

const title = 'The Enterprise Edge: A Half-Day Masterclass for Enterprise Revenue Leaders';
const description =
  'A half day for B2B revenue and customer success leaders running 50+ sellers. Bruce Scheer, Dean Edwards and Sandy Yu on getting customers to say yes, getting the deal through approval, and growing the account.';
const canonical = 'https://getenterpriseedge.com/';

/* Dated cities first and soonest first, then the ones still to be scheduled.
   The tiles used to rely on the order of the CITIES array, which was fine
   while exactly one city had a date. With two, array order put the later
   session above the sooner one and below a city with no date at all.
   Compared as Dates rather than as strings: these carry different UTC
   offsets, so comparing the ISO text would not reliably sort them. */
const ORDERED_CITIES = [
  ...CITIES.filter((c) => c.startDate).sort(
    (a, b) => new Date(a.startDate).getTime() - new Date(b.startDate).getTime()
  ),
  ...CITIES.filter((c) => !c.startDate),
];

/* "In Singapore that is The American Club" was hardcoded, which would have
   quietly become wrong the moment a venue changed or a second one was
   confirmed. Built from the data instead, as "A in City, B in City". */
const VENUES = CITIES.filter((c) => c.venue);
const VENUE_LIST = VENUES.map((c, i) => {
  const clause = `${c.venue} in ${c.city}`;
  if (i === 0) return clause;
  if (i === VENUES.length - 1) return ` and ${clause}`;
  return `, ${clause}`;
}).join('');

const jsonLd = {
  '@graph': [
    {
      '@type': 'Course',
      '@id': 'https://getenterpriseedge.com/#course',
      name: 'The Enterprise Edge',
      description,
      provider: { '@id': 'https://getenterpriseedge.com/#organization' },
      /* Only cities with a confirmed date become CourseInstance entries. A
         placeholder instance with no date and no venue is not something a
         search engine can do anything useful with, and it invites a rich
         result advertising a session that has no date. */
      hasCourseInstance: CITIES.filter((c) => c.startDate).map((c) => ({
        '@type': 'CourseInstance',
        courseMode: 'onsite',
        name: `The Enterprise Edge: ${c.city}`,
        startDate: c.startDate,
        endDate: c.endDate,
        location: {
          '@type': 'Place',
          ...(c.venue ? { name: c.venue } : {}),
          address: {
            '@type': 'PostalAddress',
            addressLocality: c.city,
            ...(c.region ? { addressRegion: c.region } : {}),
          },
        },
        ...(c.lumaUrl ? { url: c.lumaUrl } : {}),
      })),
    },
  ],
};
---
<Base
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>

  <section class="band band-ink hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">A half day for enterprise revenue leaders</p>
      <h1>Get them to say yes. Get the deal closed. Grow the account.</h1>
      <p class="lede">Three hours on the full arc of an enterprise customer, with three people who have each spent a career on one part of it.</p>
      <div class="cta-pair">
        <a class="btn" href="#dates">{HAS_DATES ? 'See dates and cities' : 'See where we are running it'}</a>
        <a class="more" href="#edges">What we cover</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band-bone band-measure">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">The problem</p>
      <h2>You can do everything right and still lose.</h2>
      <p>Strong product. A champion who genuinely wants you to win. A business case you were proud to hand over.</p>
      <p>And the deal stalls. Or it shrinks. Or it slides two quarters you cannot afford.</p>
      <p>Enterprise revenue is not one job. It is three, and they fail in completely different ways. Getting the business to believe you is a narrative and business case problem. Getting the deal approved is a commercial, legal, security and governance problem. Growing the account afterward is an outcomes and relationships problem.</p>
      <p>Most revenue organizations are strong at one of the three, adequate at the second, and quietly losing money on the third. The trouble is that the one you are worst at never sends you a report.</p>
      {/* Both figures were read off the Gartner press releases they link to,
           on 2026-09-09, and both pages state the number in those words. They
           replaced a Forrester "86% stall" and a JOLT Effect "40 to 60% no
           decision" that had been carried here unverified and are not in
           Bruce's own verified statistics bank on inspireyourbuyers.com.

           Anything that goes in this paragraph has to survive the same check:
           open the source, find the number, or do not publish it. */}
      <p>The numbers bear it out. 64% of B2B buyers cannot tell one brand&rsquo;s digital experience from another&rsquo;s (<a href="https://www.gartner.com/en/newsroom/press-releases/gartner-says-b2b-sales-organizations-need-to-give-customers-a-se" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Gartner</a>). And when the information reaching them contradicts itself, they are 153% more likely to settle for something smaller and less disruptive than they set out to buy (<a href="https://www.gartner.com/en/newsroom/press-releases/2019-07-29-gartner-reveals-new-b2b-sales-approach-to-win-in-toda" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Gartner</a>).</p>
      <p>That is the shrink, measured. It does not arrive with a reason attached.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band-bone2 band-measure" id="promise">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">What you leave with</p>
      <h2>New ideas to improve revenue performance.</h2>
      <p>Working frameworks across the whole revenue motion, not one slice of it. How you acquire customers, how you get contracts approved, and how you grow the accounts you win. Most programs take one of those three. The gaps between them are where the revenue actually goes.</p>
      <p>And the other half of the value is sitting next to you.</p>
      <p>Every seat belongs to someone running a revenue organization at your scale, working the same problems on different accounts. The cross-fertilizing is not a side effect of the format. It is half the reason we cap the room.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band-ink" id="edges">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">The three edges</p>
      <h2>Three jobs. Three hours. Three people who have done them.</h2>

      <div class="pull">
        <p><b>Bruce</b> will get your customers to say yes. <b>Dean</b> will make sure the damn deal closes. <b>Sandy</b> will help you grow the account.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="edges">
        {EDGES.map((e) => (
          <article class="edge">
            <div class="edge-num" aria-hidden="true">{e.num}</div>
            <div>
              <p class="edge-domain">{e.domain}</p>
              <h3><a href={`/${e.slug}/`}>{e.name}</a></h3>
              <p class="edge-promise">{e.promise}</p>
              <p>{e.blurb}</p>
              <ul class="edge-disciplines">
                {e.disciplines.map((d) => <li set:html={d} />)}
              </ul>
              {e.extra && <p class="fine" style="margin-top:1rem" set:html={e.extra} />}
            </div>
            <div class="edge-who">
              {/* Portrait comes from FACULTY, matched on speaker name, so a
                  photo is stored once and appears everywhere that person does. */}
              {FACULTY.find((f) => f.name === e.speaker)?.photo && (
                <img
                  src={FACULTY.find((f) => f.name === e.speaker).photo}
                  alt={e.speaker}
                  width="88"
                  height="88"
                  loading="lazy"
                />
              )}
              <b>{e.speaker}</b>
              <span>{e.job}</span>
            </div>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band-bone band-measure">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">The outcome</p>
      <h2>What it looks like when all three go your way</h2>
      <p>Your champion tells the buying group the same story you would have told it, in their own words, without you there to correct anything.</p>
      <p>Procurement opens the file and finds a case already built the way they would have built it. Security and AI governance get their answers on the first pass instead of the third. The price challenge still comes. It lands on a wider conversation about value instead of on your margin.</p>
      <p>And a year later, the review your customer runs without you is about where to expand next, not about whether to renew.</p>
      <p>That is one deal that went the distance without you in any of the meetings that decided it. It is also the only kind of deal that scales.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band-ink" id="agenda">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">The half day</p>
      <h2>A half day, and then a meal on us.</h2>
      <p class="lede">Candid conversation, working frameworks, and a room of senior leaders comparing notes on deals that are not moving.</p>

      <ul class="agenda">
        <li><b>Doors</b><span>Drinks and light networking<i>Come early. The best conversations start before the first slide.</i></span></li>
        <li><b>Hour 1</b><span>The Value Edge, with Bruce Scheer<i>Find Value, Communicate Value, Quantify Value.</i></span></li>
        <li><b>Hour 2</b><span>The Approval Edge, with Dean Edwards<i>What Procurement, Finance, Legal, InfoSec and AI governance actually evaluate.</i></span></li>
        <li><b>Hour 3</b><span>The Growth Edge, with Sandy Yu<i>Protecting the revenue you have and growing what comes next.</i></span></li>
        <li><b>The meal</b><span>Debrief, and no rush to leave<i>Bring the deals that are stuck. That is the conversation.</i></span></li>
      </ul>

      <p class="fine" style="margin-top:2rem">Twenty-five seats a city, and every one of them approved before it is confirmed. The room stays small enough for people to speak candidly about deals they would not discuss on a webinar.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band-bone band-measure">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">The room</p>
      <h2>First-class venues. The room is part of the program.</h2>
      {/* Venues listed from the data so this sentence cannot go stale or
          name a room we are no longer in. */}
      <p>We run these in the best rooms we can find: {VENUE_LIST}.</p>

      <p>That is not hospitality for its own sake. You are giving us a working morning out of a week that did not have one spare, and the room you spend it in decides whether the conversation stays polite or gets somewhere.</p>
      <p>Where a session runs into a meal, the meal is on the agenda rather than after it, because a good share of the value is in it. The people beside you are running revenue organizations at your scale, working the same problems on different accounts. An hour with them over a proper meal earns its place.</p>
      <p>A seat costs a token fee. It does not cover the room or the meal and it is not meant to. It is there because a seat that cost something is a seat that gets used, and because twenty people who turned up beats sixty who meant to.</p>
      {/* Venue photographs identify where each session is held. The site
          makes no claim that a venue hosts, sponsors or endorses the
          program, and the alt text says "the venue in" for that reason.

          Cropping happens in CSS rather than to the files. That keeps the
          Wikimedia image we distribute byte-identical to the original, which
          matters because a crop is an adaptation under CC BY-SA and would
          otherwise carry its own licensing obligation. */}
      <div class="venues">
        {CITIES.filter((c) => c.venuePhoto).map((c) => (
          <figure class="venue">
            <img
              src={c.venuePhoto}
              alt={`${c.venue}, the venue in ${c.city}`}
              loading="lazy"
              style={c.venuePhotoPosition ? `object-position:${c.venuePhotoPosition}` : undefined}
            />
            <figcaption>
              <b>{c.venue}</b>
              <span>{c.city}</span>
              {c.venuePhotoCredit && (
                <small class="credit">
                  <a href={c.venuePhotoCredit.authorUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">{c.venuePhotoCredit.text}</a>, <a href={c.venuePhotoCredit.licenceUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">{c.venuePhotoCredit.licence}</a>
                </small>
              )}
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band-bone2 band-measure">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Who should be in the room</p>
      <h2>Revenue leaders running 50 sellers or more.</h2>
      <p>CROs, VPs and SVPs of Sales, and the revenue enablement leaders who own how a large team sells. Typically B2B companies above $100 million in revenue, with around two thousand people. We make selective exceptions down to $50 million and twenty sellers.</p>
      <p>The specific person we are looking for has healthy enterprise deals stalling or quietly disappearing, and cannot see what is happening inside the customer&rsquo;s organization to cause it. Or has customers who are perfectly happy and somehow never expand.</p>
      <p>It is where our heritage is. Bruce spent his career enabling enterprise marketers and sellers at Microsoft, HP, Adobe, SAP, Oracle, Google and eBay Enterprise. Dean ran Procurement for Kaiser Permanente, Yahoo and Levi Strauss. Sandy led cloud customer insight and success at Oracle. Between us we have been on every side of the enterprise deal except yours.</p>
      <p>Bring the deals that are not moving, the accounts you are trying to grow, and the questions that do not have easy answers.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band-ink" id="dates">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Cities</p>
      <h2>{HAS_DATES ? 'Where and when' : 'Where we’re running it'}</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        {HAS_REGISTRATION
          ? 'Each city is its own session with its own registration. Pick yours to reserve a seat.'
          : 'Each city runs as its own session, in a first-class venue. Twenty-five seats, approved before they are confirmed. Registration opens city by city.'}
      </p>

      {/* Three states per city, in ORDERED_CITIES order so the next session
          leads. A tile links only when there is genuinely an event page
          behind it; a dated city without one says so rather than looking
          clickable and doing nothing. */}
      <div class="dates">
        {ORDERED_CITIES.map((c) => (
          c.lumaUrl ? (
            <a class="date is-open" href={c.lumaUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
              <b>{c.city}</b>
              <span class="when">{c.date}</span>
              {c.doors && <span class="detail">Doors {c.doors}</span>}
              {c.time && <span class="detail">{c.time}</span>}
              {c.venue && <span class="detail">{c.venue}</span>}
              {c.venueAddress && <span class="detail">{c.venueAddress}</span>}
              {c.meal && <span class="detail">{c.meal}</span>}
              {c.price && <span class="detail price">{c.price}</span>}
              <span class="go">Reserve a seat</span>
            </a>
          ) : c.date ? (
            <div class="date is-set">
              <b>{c.city}</b>
              <span class="when">{c.date}</span>
              {c.doors && <span class="detail">Doors {c.doors}</span>}
              {c.time && <span class="detail">{c.time}</span>}
              {c.venue && <span class="detail">{c.venue}</span>}
              {c.venueAddress && <span class="detail">{c.venueAddress}</span>}
              {c.meal && <span class="detail">{c.meal}</span>}
              {c.price && <span class="detail price">{c.price}</span>}
              <span class="tbd">Registration opens soon</span>
            </div>
          ) : (
            <div class="date">
              <b>{c.city}</b>
              <span class="tbd">Dates soon</span>
            </div>
          )
        ))}
      </div>

      <div class="cta-pair">
        <a class="btn" href="/assessment/">Take the assessment</a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/work-with-us/">Bring it to your team</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band-bone band-measure">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Three jobs. You are probably losing money on one of them.</h2>
      <p>The hard part is that it is rarely the one you think. The job you are worst at is the one that fails quietly, in meetings you are not in, and reaches the board deck disguised as something else.</p>
      <p class="emph">Which of the three is costing you the most right now?</p>
      <div class="cta-pair">
        <a class="btn" href="/assessment/">Find out in four minutes</a>
        <a class="more" href="/faculty/">Meet the three of us</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</Base>
